CORRECTION to my earlier notify-senechal note (the one proposing a `git fetch` before the ahead/landed computation): that hypothesis is WRONG, please do not build it as stated. Tested directly in senechal after a third FAIL: @{u} was 42088dc BOTH before and after an explicit `git fetch`, and `git show @{u}:.claude/FOCUS.md | grep -qF "<first 60 chars>"` MATCHED at that point. The remote-tracking ref was not stale. Also ruled out by reading b60bac7: `cd "$SENECHAL"` at line 58 precedes the checks at 72-76, so it is not running in the wrong repo; and scheduler cmd_commit_file only prints "committed + pushed" when the push actually returned 0 (bin/scheduler:791), so "-i reported success" is not itself a lie. VERIFIED FACTS ONLY: 3 of 3 notify-senechal invocations tonight reported FAIL; all 3 notes are on senechal origin/main afterwards, verified independently by content grep (92ebe82, 633d3c2, 42088dc). So landed() returned false at check time for a note that is present moments later, and I could not reproduce it after the fact. THE ACTUAL DEFECT, and the thing worth fixing first regardless of root cause: landed() dies WITHOUT SHOWING ITS EVIDENCE. On failure it should print what it compared -- the sha of @{u} it read, whether `git show` produced any output at all, the exact probe string, and the first matching line if any -- so the next occurrence is diagnosable instead of archaeological. As it stands the guard destroys the only state that could explain it. Second suggestion, cheap and independent: derive the probe from the commit the script just made (`git show --stat HEAD` / the inserted line) rather than `head -c 60` of the raw input, so the comparison is against what was actually written rather than what was passed in. I would rather hand you an instrumented failure than another guess.
